 "notes": "Tuning half of recommendation-holdout.json (odd positions). The other half is recommendation-holdout-test.json and must not be read while tuning. Hold-out set, labelled by hand from the catalogue itself (blocks.json names and summaries, plus a read of each candidate's summary) before any search or ranking code was looked at. No query was chosen, tuned or re-labelled by running site search or the evaluation script. Queries are new: none is copied or paraphrased from recommendation-eval.json. Mix: 40 vague outcome goals (area vague), 30 beginner phrasings, 20 typos or bad grammar, 30 outside marketing (finance and accounting, HR, teaching and study, software and data, legal paperwork, personal habits, design), 20 that name a tool (Shopify, QuickBooks, Excel, Notion, Google Ads, Slack, WordPress, GA4, Zapier, Stripe, Meta, Zendesk), 20 about AI itself. search.expect: any of these in the top 5 is a hit; the first listed is the best single answer; every listed item is one a careful person would be happy to land on. Tool-named queries whose tool is not named in the catalogue (for example QuickBooks) are labelled with the generic job item that does the work. goals.steps: each inner list is one needed step, satisfied by any slug in it. next.good: links a careful person would follow next; next.goodTasks: jobs where a next link is acceptable even if not listed. gaps: queries a visitor could plausibly type where the catalogue has no good item (checked by searching names and summaries for the topic); use them to test that weak matches are shown honestly, not to score hits. Use this file only to check that ranking changes generalise, not to tune against.",
